{"id":"delinquency-notices-and-payments","title":"Delinquency notices from the State, and City payments in the same years","question":"Which City-funded charities were sent delinquency, suspension or revocation notices by the Attorney General's registry, and what did the City pay them in those fiscal years and afterwards?","finding":"Of 95 registry records joined to a City payee, 48 were sent 93 delinquency or suspension notices since 2017. The City paid those organizations $65.2 million in the fiscal years the notices fall in, most to Low Income Investment Fund ($33.4 million, 2 notices); PRC ($5.5 million, 3 notices); Lavender Youth Recreation & Info Ctr ($3.2 million, 1 notice). 5 are shown as delinquent or revoked on 21 November 2026.","how":"The registry's own document list for each record gives the date and type of every notice (first and second delinquency notices, incomplete-and-delinquent letters, intent to suspend, revocation). Notices sent on one day count once. City payments are summed for the City fiscal year each notice date falls in (\"paid in notice years\") and for all later fiscal years (\"paid after the latest notice\"). Status is the registry's on the day the records were saved.","limits":"The notice says a delinquent charity may not solicit or disburse charitable funds; it is a State rule about charities, not a City contracting rule, and the City pays for services under contract. Most notices are cured within months by filing a late report or a fee, and the registry itself runs months behind. A fiscal year holds payments made before the notice as well as after it. Notices before 2017 are left out. Payments through a fiscal sponsor are not under the charity's own name.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization","type":"org"},{"k":"reg","t":"Registry number","type":"text"},{"k":"status","t":"Status on 21 November 2026","type":"text"},{"k":"notices","t":"Notices since 2017","type":"int"},{"k":"first","t":"First","type":"date"},{"k":